  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nAs we finish up the work week, the synoptic pattern will start to \nshift, with the persistent mid-level ridge retrograding west as a \ncut-off mid-level low slides over the Florida Peninsula. The \nmoisture won't really start to move in until over the weekend, so \nFriday will be another very warm and humid day across the area, with \nhighs in the 90s and heat index values near the triple digits. Most \nareas will be rain-free, though the eastern half of the area could \nsee a few afternoon showers and storms as subsidence from the ridge \nlowers and moisture from the low trickles in. Over the weekend, the \npattern would support continued warm and humid conditions as well as \ngradually increasing rain and thunderstorm chances as moisture pools \nover the entire area. This is by no means looking like a washout \nweekend, but we could see scattered to numerous showers and storms \neach day. High temps would remain in the upper 80s to low 90s, with \nheat index values in the mid to upper 90s for much of the area.\n\nConfidence in the forecast drops off for early next week, as a chunk \nof the guidance keeps the cut off low over the region while other \nguidance has the low get absorbed by troughing to the north and \nshifting out of the area with drier zonal flow moving in. For now, \nit is looking like the more likely solution is the persistent low \nover the area, supporting continued warm and humid conditions with \ndaily showers and storms.",
